Found while implementing the `started` clamp for #138, by surveying every card
on this board.

`started` was not the only lifecycle stamp karr wrote as a bare `YYYY-MM-DD`
date before ticket #68. `completed` was too, and it is the larger population:

    138 cards on this board
    110 carry a bare-date `completed`; 51 of those fall before their own `created`
     93 carry a bare-date `started`;   75 of those fall before their own `created`
      0 bare-date `created`, 0 bare-date `updated`
      0 cards whose `updated` precedes their `created`

Two consequences, one already live and one created by #138:

- `karr metrics` computes lead time whenever `created` and `completed` both
  parse, with no ordering check at all (that is deliberate -- see its POD on
  not clamping impossible durations). So those 51 cards already contribute
  negative lead times to the board's average lead time today.
- #138 raises `started` to `created` on 75 cards. On 42 of them the bare-date
  `completed` then falls *before* the new `started`: their cycle time reads
  negative where before the clamp it was merely absent. `karr repair` names
  those cards on a line of its own and in `started_clamped_over_completed`
  under `--json`, because the clamp creates that ordering rather than
  inheriting it -- but it does not fix it.

Not decided here, and deliberately not decided inside #138:

- whether `completed` gets a clamp of its own, and to what. Unlike `started`
  there is no single defensible value: `created` is too early (the work was
  not finished the moment it was filed) and `started` asserts zero cycle time.
  End-of-day (`T23:59:59Z`) is the only value the bare date actually bounds,
  but it is an invention.
- whether the honest answer is instead that this board's history before #68 is
  day-granular, and every reader of it has to say so rather than pretend to
  hours. See the companion ticket on `karr metrics`.

Storage/migration side is karr-ref-worker; the reader-side rule is
karr-board-worker.
Resolved in 007c7c4 per maintainer decision: no clamp, no migration, karr repair untouched. The reader side was made honest instead -- negative_lead_samples under --json plus a note in both other renderings, kept separate from unusable_timestamps, and a POD section stating that boards before #68 are day-granular so an hour-precise average is finer than its data. Measured on this board: 51 of 138 lead samples negative, averaging -10.1h; dropping them would move the printed average from 6h39m to 16h28m. Regression test t/143-metrics-negative-lead.t.
